---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I specify chilli powder?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Name five things: the variety (or SHU window), heat level in SHU, mesh size, moisture, and colour (ASTA) if relevant. A complete specification names all five — heat, colour and flavour vary independently."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does 60–80 mesh mean for chilli powder?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Mesh is particle size. 60–80 mesh is the standard fine grind for chilli and paprika powder — even dispersion and smooth mouthfeel. Coarser (20–40) is for flakes; finer (100+) for smooth sauces."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does batch consistency matter?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Because heat is non-linear and variety-dependent. A named variety plus a precise SHU window — not a generic 'chilli powder' — is what keeps a sauce or seasoning consistent from one production run to the next."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Specify Chilli Powder: SHU, Mesh & Moisture | Tenda Peppers"
  description="How to specify chilli powder — SHU heat, mesh size, moisture and ASTA colour. A buyer's guide to a complete specification."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-01"
  dateModified="2026-09-01"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Specify Chilli Powder
    </nav>
    <h1>How to Specify Chilli Powder: SHU, Mesh &amp; Moisture</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A chilli powder specification is five numbers — variety, heat (SHU), mesh, moisture and colour.
      Miss one and you risk a batch that looks right but performs wrong.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> A chilli powder specification is five numbers: variety, heat (SHU), mesh, moisture and colour (ASTA). Miss one and you get a batch that looks right but performs wrong — because heat, colour and texture are independent and each is controlled by a different number.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The five things to specify</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Variety (or SHU window)</strong> — "Sanying, 20,000–50,000 SHU", not "hot chilli"</li>
      <li><strong>Heat level (SHU)</strong> — the precise window, because heat is non-linear</li>
      <li><strong>Mesh size</strong> — 60–80 mesh standard, adjust for flakes or smooth sauces</li>
      <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — under 13% for shelf stability</li>
      <li><strong>Colour (ASTA)</strong> — if colour matters in your application</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why "chilli powder" is not a specification</h2>
    <p>
      Heat, colour and flavour vary independently between varieties. Two powders can both be "chilli
      powder" yet sit 40,000 SHU apart — one a mild seasoning, the other an extreme-heat product. The
      variety and the SHU window are what pin the product down.
    </p>
    <p>
      See our <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> and
      <a href="/heat-levels/extra-hot/">extra hot</a> pages for the variety-to-SHU mapping, or the
      full <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/heat-level-reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">heat level reference</a> in the Knowledge Hub.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Mesh and moisture: the two people forget</h2>
    <p>
      Mesh controls particle size — how evenly heat and colour disperse, and how the powder feels in
      a sauce. Moisture (under 13%) controls shelf life and clumping. Both are cheap to get right and
      expensive to get wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">A worked example specification</h2>
    <p>
      Here is what a complete, ready-to-quote chilli powder specification looks like in one line:
    </p>
    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Example:</strong> "Sanying chilli powder, 20,000–50,000 SHU, 60–80 mesh, moisture ≤13%, ASTA ≥100, packed 25 kg kraft bags, export grade."
    </div>
    <p>
      Everything is pinned: the variety fixes heat, colour and flavour; the mesh fixes texture; the
      moisture fixes shelf life; the packaging fixes the supply chain. A supplier who sees this can
      quote accurately; a supplier who sees only "chilli powder" has to guess — and a guess is a batch
      that changes every order.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The three mistakes that change your batch</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specifying "hot" or "mild"</strong> — no SHU window means the supplier picks a variety, and it will drift.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting moisture</strong> — it is invisible at quotation time and ruins the batch later.</li>
      <li><strong>Specifying colour and heat but not variety</strong> — you cannot optimise all three independently; the variety sets the trade-off.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How do I specify chilli powder?</h3>
      <p>Name five things: the variety (or SHU window), heat level in SHU, mesh size, moisture, and colour (ASTA) if relevant. A complete specification names all five — heat, colour and flavour vary independently.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What does 60–80 mesh mean for chilli powder?</h3>
      <p>Mesh is particle size. 60–80 mesh is the standard fine grind for chilli and paprika powder — even dispersion and smooth mouthfeel. Coarser (20–40) is for flakes; finer (100+) for smooth sauces.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does batch consistency matter?</h3>
      <p>Because heat is non-linear and variety-dependent. A named variety plus a precise SHU window — not a generic 'chilli powder' — is what keeps a sauce or seasoning consistent from one production run to the next.</p>
    </div>

